WEBVTT

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:07.262
This video uses the Genes and Sequences demo project.

00:00:07.262 --> 00:00:12.703
Choose Help, then Demo Projects, and click Download and Open beside it.

00:00:12.703 --> 00:00:19.371
It holds NG_000007.3, the NCBI reference record for the human beta-globin gene cluster.

00:00:19.371 --> 00:00:22.819
The video Your first project shows each step.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:30.544
Click NG_000007.3 under Reference Sequences, then click Focus to show it in the viewer.

00:00:30.544 --> 00:00:39.757
It holds HBB, the gene for beta-globin, one of the protein chains of hemoglobin, which carries oxygen in red blood cells.

00:00:39.757 --> 00:00:45.620
Annotated features, such as genes and coding sequences, show as colored bars.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:54.066
A codon is three bases in a gene's coding sequence that specify one amino acid.

00:00:54.066 --> 00:01:05.968
Choose Sequence, then Go to Location, type the record name and the codon's first and last bases, 70,613 to 70,615, and click Go.

00:01:05.968 --> 00:01:09.409
The viewer zooms to three bases, GAG.

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:18.492
The Translation row below the bases shows the amino acid each codon specifies, as a one-letter code.

00:01:18.492 --> 00:01:23.440
GAG specifies glutamate, also called glutamic acid, written E.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:29.560
In sickle hemoglobin, HbS, the middle A of this codon is a T.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:36.885
GTG specifies valine, so amino acid 6 of beta-globin becomes valine instead of glutamate.

00:01:36.885 --> 00:01:44.128
When oxygen is low, HbS forms long fibers that bend red blood cells into a sickle shape.

00:01:44.128 --> 00:01:48.078
Sickle cell disease needs HbS from both parents.

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:53.480
An annotation is your own labeled mark on a stretch of sequence.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:59.796
Drag across the three bases, choose Sequence, then Add Annotation, type a name, and click Add.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.588
LGE saves your annotation in the project.

00:02:03.588 --> 00:02:10.341
Go back to the codon and it shows on the Manual Annotations track, a row of the viewer under the bases.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:17.051
Next, learn to check the quality of a sequencing run, on the Videos page of the website.
